.he "scsiformat (1)               10/11/26                scsiformat (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
scsiformat
- format a SCSI disk through the NCR 5380
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
scsiformat [ -c cylinders ] [ -h heads ] [ -s sectors ] device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Scsiformat formats a SCSI disk.  A SCSI disk formats itself: the host
sends FORMAT UNIT and the target does the work, so there is no track
walk here the way mwformat (1) and hdformat (1) have - one command, and
the drive is blank.  What scsiformat then does is the part the drive
cannot: it writes the disk label, the geometry and the prototype slice
table, into block 0, so the freshly formatted drive can be opened and
partitioned.
.sp
The device named must be the whole-disk slice, the
.bd
c
node: scsi0c, scsi1c and so on, exactly as label (1) names them.
.sp
The geometry is the target's own and is not a table's, so there is no
model flag.  -c, -h and -s say it outright, and without them the
geometry already in the label is kept, so a disk can be re-formatted
without re-typing its shape:
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
-c cylinders	The track count.
.sp
.ti -10
-h heads	The head count.
.sp
.ti -10
-s sectors	The sectors per track.
.sp
.in -10
A fresh disk has no label, so it has no geometry to keep: give all
three.  A re-format of a labelled disk with none of them keeps the
label's numbers.
.sp
The label scsiformat writes carries the prototype slice table: track 0
holds the boot and the label, slice a is the biggest filesystem that
fits, b is swap, and d through h are filesystems until the drive runs
out.  The boot is not written - mkfs -i puts one there and rewrites the
label's numbers when it does.  A freshly formatted disk still needs
mkfs (1) before it can be mounted.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
mwformat (1), hdformat (1), djformat (1), label (1), mkfs (1)
